          ],
          "content": "Journey Inside the Atom"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 157"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.093,
            0.924,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.319,
                0.261,
                0.711,
                0.277
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.16: Journey of the development of atomic models"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.295,
            0.329,
            0.317
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "At a Glance"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.328,
            0.532,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "y Atoms are the building blocks of matter."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.352,
            0.754,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": "J. J. Thomson proposed that in an atom, electrons are embedded in a positively charged sphere."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.392,
            0.756,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "Rutherford’s model described the atom as mostly empty space, with a dense, positively charged nucleus at its centre and electrons orbiting it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.449,
            0.754,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "Niels Bohr’s model proposed that electrons move in fixed energy levels (shells) around the nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.489,
            0.677,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "The shells of an atom are named as K, L, M, N, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.512,
            0.753,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "y James Chadwick discovered the presence of neutrons in the atom."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.535,
            0.753,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "The three subatomic particles of an atom are electrons, protons, and neutrons."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.575,
            0.755,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "If the outermost shell of an atom has an octet of electrons (or two electrons in case of helium), the atom is stable and largely unreactive."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.632,
            0.755,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "Valency is the combining capacity of an atom. It is equal to the number of electrons which can be gained, lost or shared by an atom to achieve a stable configuration."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.689,
            0.755,
            0.724
          ],
          "content": "The atomic number of an element is equal to the number of protons in its nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.728,
            0.755,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "The mass number of an atom is equal to the total number of nucleons (protons and neutrons) in its nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.769,
            0.754,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "Isotopes are atoms of same element that have the same atomic number but different mass numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.808,
            0.754,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "The average atomic mass of an element is calculated based on the relative abundance of its isotopes in nature."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.849,
            0.754,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": "Isobars are atoms of different elements with the same mass number but different atomic numbers."
        },
        {
